From: Waltteri Hooli <1420194+wahooli@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] local: usb: typec: mux: ps883x: reset the retimer when the sink re-asserts HPD

On the Yoga Slim 7x, with a Samsung C49G95T at 5120x1440@120 (HBR3 x4)
behind a USB-C dock and a DP KVM, the link occasionally loses lock on all
four lanes. About ten seconds later the PS8830's LTTPR caps fall back from
HBR3 x4 (rev 2.0) to HBR2 x1 (rev 1.2). CONN_STATUS still reads 03/87/00,
so the chip has not been reset, and the caps stay degraded until a replug.
msm DP clamps the link to them: black before 0235/0236, a low mode after.

Toggling HPD_LEVEL through a low period does not clear the fallback. A
replug does, and the part of a replug that reaches the retimer is
ps883x_reset() (rails and reset line) followed by a fresh configure.

The sink answers the dead link by dropping HPD, which pmic_glink passes on
as a DP event with DP_STATUS_HPD_STATE clear, and raises it again a second
or two later. Treat that re-assertion like a replug: reset the retimer and
let the configure below bring it back up with its caps restored, before
pmic_glink notifies DRM of the new HPD state.

Only a high -> low -> high sequence while the retimer is running counts,
so the first HPD after plug-in and repeated IRQ_HPD events do not reset it.
Skip it when the port also carries USB 3 (pin assignment D), which the
reset would drop.

--- a/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
@@ -63,6 +63,8 @@
 
 	enum typec_orientation orientation;
 	bool in_reset;
+	bool dp_hpd;		/* sink HPD level at the last DP event */
+	bool dp_hpd_dropped;	/* went low after having been high */
 };
 
 static int ps883x_enable_vregs(struct ps883x_retimer *retimer)
@@ -198,6 +200,7 @@
 
 static int ps883x_set(struct ps883x_retimer *retimer, struct typec_retimer_state *state)
 {
+	const struct typec_displayport_data *dp_data = NULL;
 	struct typec_thunderbolt_data *tb_data;
 	const struct enter_usb_data *eudo_data;
 	int cfg0 = CONN_STATUS_0_CONNECTION_PRESENT;
@@ -211,6 +214,7 @@
 	if (state->alt) {
 		switch (state->alt->svid) {
 		case USB_TYPEC_DP_SID:
+			dp_data = state->data;
 			cfg1 |= CONN_STATUS_1_DP_CONNECTED |
 				CONN_STATUS_1_DP_HPD_LEVEL;
 
@@ -272,6 +276,33 @@
 		}
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * After a link loss the LTTPR can fall back to HBR2 x1 caps (rev 1.2)
+	 * and keep them until the chip is reset; toggling HPD_LEVEL does not
+	 * clear it. The sink answers a dead link by dropping HPD, so treat its
+	 * return like a replug. Not with USB 3 on the port, which would drop too.
+	 */
+	if (dp_data) {
+		bool hpd = dp_data->status & DP_STATUS_HPD_STATE;
+
+		if (!hpd && retimer->dp_hpd)
+			retimer->dp_hpd_dropped = true;
+
+		if (hpd && retimer->dp_hpd_dropped && !retimer->in_reset &&
+		    !(cfg0 & CONN_STATUS_0_USB_3_1_CONNECTED)) {
+			dev_info(&retimer->client->dev,
+				 "sink re-asserted HPD, resetting the retimer\n");
+			ps883x_reset(retimer);
+		}
+
+		if (hpd)
+			retimer->dp_hpd_dropped = false;
+		retimer->dp_hpd = hpd;
+	} else {
+		retimer->dp_hpd = false;
+		retimer->dp_hpd_dropped = false;
+	}
+
 	return ps883x_configure(retimer, cfg0, cfg1, cfg2, reset);
 }
 
